   and a future OrcaSlicer plugin will wrap them, so no web or slicer imports. #}
{{ config(model_type="coding_agent", agent_step_limit=8, promptfiles=["a1_start", "a1_end", "real_a1"]) }}
{{ ref('3_a_build_rules') }}

Your step: create the `autoeject/` core package.

0. Copy the reference files into the tree unchanged (byte for byte; later steps and
   tests read them at these paths): `a1_start/a1_start.gcode` -> `profiles/a1_start.gcode`,
   `a1_end/a1_end.gcode` -> `profiles/a1_end.gcode`, `real_a1/real_a1.gcode.3mf` ->
   `fixtures/real_a1.gcode.3mf` (all under `.promptmap_inputs/`).

1. `autoeject/ejectability.py`: `check(stl_bytes) -> Report` using trimesh.
   Implement the spec's ejectability_rules exactly (thresholds as named constants).
   Report and its rules are dataclasses with attributes (verdict pass|warn|fail;
   rules: id, measured, threshold, verdict, reason) plus `.to_dict()` for JSON.
2. `autoeject/end_block.py`:
   - `build_end_block(params) -> str`: `profiles/a1_end.gcode` with the spec's
     eject_block_params (bed heat/cool temps, hold times, cycle count) substituted,
     slicer placeholders left in for the slicer. Invalid params raise ValueError.
   - `evaluate_end_block(block, values) -> str`: fill the placeholders this template
     uses (`max_layer_z`, `first_layer_center_no_wipe_tower[1]`, `spiral_mode`,
     `print_sequence`, and its `{if}/{else}/{endif}` blocks) from a dict, for files that
     were sliced elsewhere. Any other placeholder raises ValueError (fail closed).
3. Tests with STL fixtures generated in-test by trimesh (cube 40 mm, cube 20 mm,
   tall thin rod, part off the bed). A 20 mm cube must NOT fail. Evaluating with
   max_layer_z=20.08 takes the nozzle-push branch (`G1 Z1`); 60 takes `G1 Z20`.
